From f54d3e6ba3edf9634be1e3058d9d519a1fd3de1b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?=
 <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 21:15:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] camera: give the viewfinder its own resolution, and lower it
 when it is slow

The source's capsfilter is shared by the viewfinder and by still capture, so
the two have always had one resolution between them: whatever is smooth enough
to preview is also what gets saved, and whatever is worth saving makes the
preview crawl. On a phone with a 12 MP sensor and a software ISP the two
numbers are nowhere near each other - measured on a Fairphone 3, the pipeline
sustains about 6 fps at the sensor's full 4032x3024 and about 30 fps at
1920x1080.

Separate them. Still capture is pinned to the sensor's largest resolution
through camerabin's image-capture-caps, and the viewfinder gets its own
viewfinder-caps, starting at the largest resolution that is not more than
1920x1080 - a phone screen cannot show more, and every pixel above that is
debayered in software for nothing.

Then measure rather than assume: a pad probe counts the buffers that actually
reach the video sink, and if fewer than 20 frames per second arrive for two
consecutive two-second windows, the viewfinder drops to the next smaller
resolution the camera offers and the pipeline restarts. It never drops below
640x480 and never raises the resolution again, so it settles instead of
oscillating, and it says what it did in the log either way.

The saved picture is unaffected by all of it, which is the point.
